WindowPane development ledger

Status: DONE
Milestone: Traceable first-preview and visual-guide development
Dependencies: None
Purpose: Explain actions, effects, reasons, mechanisms and evidence.

Tasks:
- [x] Preserve the rationale for moving from a portal widget to a visual mirror.
- [x] Append implementation and qualification outcomes as they actually occur.

Acceptance criteria:
- Public history contains no credentials, private portal data, or raw conversation.
- Design, implementation, technical verification and human acceptance are distinct.

Completion evidence:
- Native source, build commands, focused tests and actual synthetic UI/pixel
  artifacts exist. See 04_QUALIFICATION.todo for exact observations and limits.

Completion log:
- 2026-11-06: Chose a generic local visual mirror instead of a site client.
  Effect: authentication stays entirely with the source application.
  Reason: the desired utility must not require enterprise sign-in or cookies.
  Mechanism: user-selected Windows capture plus native per-pixel composition.
  Trade-off: the source remains open and responsible for refreshing its data.
- 2026-11-06: Chose explicit pixel composition rather than basic DWM thumbnails.
  Reason: color-keying needs per-pixel alpha, not just a cropped live thumbnail.
  Native capture and CPU readback costs require actual measurements; no promise
  of zero overhead or full application compatibility has been made.
- 2026-11-06: First compilation exposed SDK namespace/header issues and a
  PowerShell argument-parsing issue in the SDK version switch. Qualified the
  C++/WinRT and ABI namespaces, included DWM interop, and quoted the CMake flag.
  The /W4 /WX gate also caught a lossy diagnostic string conversion; UTF-8
  conversion now uses winrt::to_string instead of narrowing wchar_t characters.
- 2026-11-06: First native capture/UI run passed real color-keying and desktop
  blending but timed out after restoring a minimized fixture. Windows had
  queued a differently sized animation frame. A frame's size is not sufficient
  evidence of a true source resize: current normal-window geometry now decides
  invalidation, and transient mismatched frames are discarded.
- 2026-11-06: Actual controls-image inspection found dark checkbox text and an
  incompletely repainted lower section. Native checkbox theming now follows
  the app's explicit colors; the owned-window capture waits for and repaints
  child controls. The new image visibly includes recovery buttons and shortcuts.
- 2026-11-06: Added guarded synthetic integration coverage across snipping,
  color picking, resizing/snap targets, source lifecycle and recovery. The
  harness refuses any non-fixture source HWND before capture and refuses screen
  sample points obscured by unrelated windows.
- 2026-11-06: First preview qualified locally with 18 algorithm cases, 26 native
  integration checks, native startup/tray recovery, formatting and whitespace
  checks. The executable is about 0.4MB, not an installer. A five-second
  180x110 fixture observed about 46MB working set and 0.62% of one logical core;
  these are bounded observations, not promises for every source or GPU.
- 2026-11-06: Added focused chaptered guides, algorithm/design explanations and
  original synthetic figures. No huge book/PDF/atlas, installer, account
  integration, commit, push or release was implied by following the manual.
- 2026-11-06: User requested a detailed README with many GIFs/screenshots. Added
  an explicitly invoked --demo-assets harness that uses only its owned fixture
  and controls, plus a Python/Pillow documentation-only encoder. Four GIFs show
  captured source repaint, opacity events, sampled color removal and aspect fit.
  Nine PNGs show real controls states and annotated native-output references.
  Reading cards/checkerboards are not application chrome; paced animations are
  not mouse recordings or frame-rate/latency measurements.
- 2026-11-06: Normal preview was already running, causing linker LNK1104. Used
  isolated build\media rather than stop the user's app. Extended demo teardown
  initially stalled with active capture; explicitly releasing capture before
  destroying its owned source fixed it. Standard native tests still cover source
  closure separately. Demo export passed 27 checks; both standard CTest targets
  passed. Decoded animation frames change as expected; controls/montage/native
  states and encoded samples visually reviewed. No release or publication.
- 2026-11-06: Encoded-GIF review caught incorrect dark foreground hues because
  the initial shared palette only sampled each sequence's first state. Built
  the palette from all states, re-encoded and reinspected. Every decoded frame's
  native-output area now stays within 12/255 per-channel error of its full-color
  reference; a changing-frame check alone would not have caught the color issue.
